(5905384, 859125, 65319, '<td valign="top">Jeb...I am one of your two 10''s!  I just loved this photo.  Well done and underrated IMHO!</td>', 'Jeb...I am one of your two 10''s!  I just loved this photo.  Well done and underrated IMHO!', '2010-03-15 12:30:31.000000', NULL, 0),
(5905403, 859138, 59029, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>I love to see Lensbaby shots, they are so cool.  Very nice surreal feel to the shot with the blur and soft light.  I was disappointed to see this finish so low, especially in a Fine Art challenge.  I think this image has plenty of artisitic merit...however, it does lack the dpc-friendly hit of saturation that voters love.<br/> <br/>Lighting is soft and natural, the dof especially coming in from the left is very effective in bringing that daisy forward. I do agree that a tighter crop on the right may have helped strengthen composition. The lit diagonal area adds a sense of place.<br/> <br/>Overall an unconventional shot, but I like to see the boundaries being pushed.  Keep up the good work and oob thinking!<br/> <br/>Feel free to PM me with any questions,<br/> <br/>Susan</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! I love to see Lensbaby shots, they are so cool.  Very nice surreal feel to the shot with the blur and soft light.  I was disappointed to see this finish so low, especially in a Fine Art challenge.  I think this image has plenty of artisitic merit...however, it does lack the dpc-friendly hit of saturation that voters love. Lighting is soft and natural, the dof especially coming in from the left is very effective in bringing that daisy forward. I do agree that a tighter crop on the right may have helped strengthen composition. The lit diagonal area adds a sense of place. Overall an unconventional shot, but I like to see the boundaries being pushed.  Keep up the good work and oob thinking! Feel free to PM me with any questions, Susan', '2010-03-15 12:37:38.000000', NULL, 0),
(5905414, 858595, 62357, '<td valign="top">Oh I just loved this one.  I can''t put  my finger on it, but it worked well for me.  I was one of your 9s.  I really liked it and think it''s a crying shame that it only scored 5.1  Sorry for that.</td>', 'Oh I just loved this one.  I can''t put  my finger on it, but it worked well for me.  I was one of your 9s.  I really liked it and think it''s a crying shame that it only scored 5.1  Sorry for that.', '2010-03-15 12:45:22.000000', NULL, 0),
(5905415, 859248, 65319, '<td valign="top">My only 10 to make the Top 10!  I thought this one might be from <img alt="'' . substr(''//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21.gif'', strrpos(''//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21.gif'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21.gif"/> <a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/profile.php?USER_ID=22301" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">JDannels</a>.  Congrats on your high placement, Ursula.  The presence of your excellence is quite comforting somehow.  ;-)</td>', 'My only 10 to make the Top 10!  I thought this one might be from  JDannels.  Congrats on your high placement, Ursula.  The presence of your excellence is quite comforting somehow.  ;-)', '2010-03-15 12:46:33.000000', NULL, 0),
(5905417, 859137, 59029, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>A little bit of glare visible on the whatsit (bridge?) where the strings attach does tend to draw the eye.  Looks like you strummed the bottom two strings, I think it adds nicely to a bokeh-ish effect. As always, there are going to be people who love or hate extreme dof.  The title''s quite good...and hey aren''t you in the same basic area that Anvil comes from? (I''m not a metalhead but yes I''ve seen the movie! :-)<br/> <br/>The 18-55 kit lens isn''t a bad choice for what is essentially a macro.  I am guessing you shot handheld; this would have been a good time to break out the tripod and close down the fstop and leave the shutter open.  A lower ISO may have helped reduce the glare too.  I would have tried a lower, and higher, pov too to add more interest to the shot as it is a bit static.<br/> <br/>Otherwise, you finished quite well in a challenge that has all manner of unorthodox entries.  Keep shooting!<br/> <br/>Feel free to PM me with any questions,<br/> <br/>Susan</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! A little bit of glare visible on the whatsit (bridge?) where the strings attach does tend to draw the eye.  Looks like you strummed the bottom two strings, I think it adds nicely to a bokeh-ish effect. As always, there are going to be people who love or hate extreme dof.  The title''s quite good...and hey aren''t you in the same basic area that Anvil comes from? (I''m not a metalhead but yes I''ve seen the movie! :-) The 18-55 kit lens isn''t a bad choice for what is essentially a macro.  I am guessing you shot handheld; this would have been a good time to break out the tripod and close down the fstop and leave the shutter open.  A lower ISO may have helped reduce the glare too.  I would have tried a lower, and higher, pov too to add more interest to the shot as it is a bit static. Otherwise, you finished quite well in a challenge that has all manner of unorthodox entries.  Keep shooting! Feel free to PM me with any questions, Susan', '2010-03-15 12:47:05.000000', NULL, 0),
